# MASTER BUILD PROMPT — Production-Ready AI Restaurant Operations SaaS

## 1. Your role and mission

Act as a principal SaaS architect, senior full-stack engineer, database architect, AI agent engineer, security engineer, DevOps engineer, product designer, and QA lead.

Your mission is to design, implement, test, secure, and prepare a **real, production-grade, multi-tenant restaurant management SaaS platform powered by an AI operations agent**.

The product serves independent restaurants, cafés, food businesses, restaurant groups, and multi-branch operators. It combines restaurant operations, purchasing, inventory, customers, tips, staff communication, meetings, reviews, reservations, sales integrations, automated workflows, analytics, an AI assistant, and restaurant website services in one connected platform.

This is a real commercial product, not a tutorial, demonstration, proof of concept, wireframe, mockup, or disposable MVP.

### Absolute delivery rules

1. Inspect the existing repository, tools, dependencies, hosting environment, and available credentials before making architectural decisions. Preserve useful existing work.
2. Implement the real application end to end. Do not stop after a plan, architecture diagram, landing page, or partially functional dashboard.
3. Never use fake API responses, mock payment confirmations, simulated integrations, fake AI answers, non-persistent forms, dead buttons, or fabricated analytics in production.
4. Every completed feature must have a real backend implementation, persistent data where appropriate, authorization, validation, loading states, error handling, and tests.
5. Use current official documentation to verify third-party API contracts, payment integration requirements, hosting limits, and service pricing.
6. Where credentials or external approvals are required, implement the actual integration and its configuration workflow. Clearly report the missing external prerequisite. Never represent the integration as live until it is genuinely operational.
7. Work continuously through implementation, testing, fixing, and verification. Do not deliver only a task list or tell the user to finish essential programming manually.
8. Make reasonable product decisions without unnecessary clarification. Put adjustable business rules in configuration instead of hardcoding uncertain assumptions.
9. Never claim tests passed, payments work, backups exist, or deployment succeeded without evidence.
10. Prioritize correctness, security, maintainability, real-world usability, and operational reliability over superficial feature counts.

